I ran into a situation when the core.trace_state was left in a bad state after a failing test. Here is a simple repro:
  def test_leaking_trace_stack(self):
    assert not core.trace_state.trace_stack.upward   # Assert clean trace_state
    def func(x):
      raise ValueError(x)
    try:
      jax.jvp(func, (0.,), (0.,))
    except:
      print(core.trace_state.trace_stack.upward)  # We should have a clean trace_state

The above test will print a non-empty trace_state (with a JVPTrace).
I think that this comes from the following use of generators within context managers.
@lu.transformation
def jvpfun(instantiate, primals, tangents):
  with new_master(JVPTrace) as master:
    out_primals, out_tangents = yield (master, primals, tangents), {}
    del master
  ...

Since we yield from the body context manager, if an exception is raised the context manager will not be on the stack and it won't be cleaned up by the context manager. Instead, the cleanup will run later when the context manager is garbage collected. By then, the next test may have started already and will use the wrong trace_state.
Perhaps we need to review the linear_util to catch exceptions and pass them to the generators?
